  f"We thank the Reviewer; this comment led to an important correction. On re-examining the code we found that the random seed had not been propagated to MDFS in the submitted analysis, so the two runs per fold were in fact identical and the reported stability was illusory. We therefore (i) performed a seed-stability analysis in which MDFS was run with 20 seeds on every LOCO fold and consensus sets built from two disjoint blocks of seeds were compared for all combinations of number of runs and consensus threshold (Supplementary Table 5), and (ii) re-ran the entire analysis with ten independent MDFS runs per fold, retaining a pair if it was among the top 2% of pairs by joint information gain in at least six of the ten runs. With these settings the consensus sets of the two seed blocks agree with a mean Jaccard index of {N['jac']['taxa'][0]:.2f} for taxa and {N['jac']['combined'][0]:.2f} for combined features, against {N['jac']['taxa'][1]:.2f} and {N['jac']['combined'][1]:.2f} for two runs; for functional features the agreement is lower ({N['jac']['functions'][0]:.2f}), which we now state as a limitation. The same ten-run consensus (pair retained if significant in at least six of ten runs) is applied in the extended-cohort analysis. All results, figures and tables in the revised manuscript are from this rerun; the classification performance was essentially unchanged (all mean AUC differences below 0.01), whereas the number of stable pairs increased (the 2% cutoff yields about twice as many consensus pairs per fold as the previous 1%) and several extended-cohort findings changed (see our responses to comment 12 and to Reviewer 2, comment 4).",
