  ("We thank the Reviewer. MDFS discretises each feature into two bins at a cut point drawn at random within an admissible range, which is why the pair selection has a random component and why we use repeated runs with a consensus rule (response to comment 6). We have added a sensitivity analysis on one LOCO fold (Supplementary Table 7): "
   + (f"widening the admissible cut range to the one-dimensional default or halving it changed the set of synergistic pairs by about as much as a change of random seed (Jaccard index with the default setting {_ds.set_index('config').loc['range_1D', 'jaccard_all_vs_default']:.2f} and {_ds.set_index('config').loc['range_half', 'jaccard_all_vs_default']:.2f}, versus {_ds.set_index('config').loc['default_seed43', 'jaccard_all_vs_default']:.2f} between two seeds), whereas using three bins per feature changed the pair set more substantially (Jaccard {_ds.set_index('config').loc['divisions_2', 'jaccard_all_vs_default']:.2f}), while the joint information gains of the pairs found in both settings remained strongly correlated (Spearman {_ds.set_index('config').loc['divisions_2', 'spearman_total_IG_shared_pairs']:.2f}). " if _ds is not None else "")
   + "In the course of this work we also found and corrected an inconsistency in our script: the 1D and 2D information gains had been computed with different random cut points, so part of the apparent 2D gain reflected the change of cut rather than the partner feature. In the revised analysis the 1D, 2D and pair-level computations share one seed and one cut range, so that each feature is binned identically in all steps and the joint information gain of a pair decomposes exactly into the 1D gain of the base feature and the gain added by the partner. All results were recomputed with the corrected script."),
